>
  <p>
    Group chats are where the funniest and most dramatic conversations happen, which is why
    they're the format most chat stories and memes use. They're also the format most fake-chat
    tools handle badly — one colour for every sender, no "added to the group" lines, no member
    count in the header.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's how to build a proper one: group name, member count, coloured sender names, join
    events mid-conversation and a group photo. It takes about two minutes and it's free, with no
    watermark and no signup.
  </p>

  <h2>The one rule: more than one other person</h2>
  <p>
    There's no "group mode" toggle to find. A chat becomes a group automatically the moment your
    script contains <strong>more than one person who isn't you</strong>. Three named speakers
    where one of them is your character means a group; two speakers means a private chat.
  </p>
  <p>
    The moment it flips, you get all the group furniture at once — the member count in the
    header, coloured sender names above each message, group event chips, and a group photo
    instead of a contact photo.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Write a script with three or more people</h2>
  <p>
    Open the <a href="/editor?platform=whatsapp">editor</a> and write the conversation as plain
    text, one line per message, <code>Name: message</code>. Or click the
    <strong>Launch Room</strong> premade script to start from a working group and edit it.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-script.webp" alt="The script panel in Easy Chat Maker containing a group chat script with System lines, a Date line, several speakers and a Reaction line" loading="lazy" width="623" height="414" />
    <figcaption>A group script: System lines for events, Date for a day chip, one line per message.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Four special line types do the work that plain messages can't:</p>
  <pre><code>System: Aisha created group "Launch Room 🚀"
System: Aisha added Mateo, Priya, Kenji
Date: Monday
Aisha: Morning team, quick launch check-in.
Mateo: Landing page is ready. I just need final hero copy.
Reaction: 🔥
Kenji: Payment test passed in Japan and Germany.</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>System:</code> — a group event chip (created, added, renamed, left).</li>
    <li><code>Date:</code> — a date or day separator.</li>
    <li><code>Reaction:</code> — attaches an emoji to the message directly above it.</li>
    <li><code>Name:</code> — an ordinary message from that person.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 2 — Pick which character is you</h2>
  <p>
    Under the script box, the <strong>Your character</strong> dropdown lists everyone the script
    mentions. Whoever you pick gets the outgoing bubbles — green, right-aligned, with read
    receipts. Everyone else appears on the left with their name and avatar.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-your-character.webp" alt="The 'Your character' dropdown in the editor with a script character selected" loading="lazy" width="617" height="123" />
    <figcaption>Pick who "you" are — this decides which side each message lands on.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    This is also what decides group-vs-private. Pick your character, then hit
    <strong>Generate Chat</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3 — Name the group</h2>
  <p>
    If your script opens with a <code>System:</code> line in the form
    <code>X created group "Name"</code>, the name in quotes becomes the chat title automatically —
    emoji included. Without that line you'll get "Group Chat", which you can click and retype in
    the header.
  </p>
  <p>
    The line underneath fills in with the member count for the platform you're on — "5 members"
    on WhatsApp, "5 members, 2 online" on Telegram, "5 Members" on Discord. It's editable text
    like everything else, so change it to whatever suits.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-header.webp" alt="A WhatsApp group chat header showing the group name 'Launch Room' with a rocket emoji, a group avatar and a 5 members subtitle" loading="lazy" width="540" height="162" />
    <figcaption>Group name pulled from the script, member count filled in automatically.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Click the round avatar in the header to upload a group photo. Until you do, it shows the
    group's initials on a coloured circle.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4 — Group events</h2>
  <p>
    The grey pills running down the middle of the feed are what sell a group chat as real. Every
    <code>System:</code> line in your script becomes one, and they can say anything — created the
    group, added someone, changed the photo, left.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-events.webp" alt="A WhatsApp group chat showing 'Aisha created group Launch Room' and 'Aisha added Mateo, Priya, Kenji' event chips followed by a date chip" loading="lazy" width="540" height="353" />
    <figcaption>Group event chips and a date separator at the top of the conversation.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Step 5 — Coloured sender names</h2>
  <p>
    In a group, each person's name appears above their first message in a run, in their own
    colour — exactly like the real app. The colour is derived from that person's identity in the
    chat, so it stays the same for them from the first message to the last. Their avatar circle
    is coloured separately, by position in the group, so the initials circles stay distinct even
    when two people's name colours are close.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-sender-names.webp" alt="Group chat messages from different senders, each with their name shown above the bubble in a distinct colour" loading="lazy" width="603" height="312" />
    <figcaption>Each sender gets their own colour, shown above their first message in a run.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Step 6 — Add people mid-conversation</h2>
  <p>
    Real groups grow while people are talking, and that detail is worth copying. Drop another
    <code>System:</code> line partway down your script and the new person joins right there — the
    event chip appears mid-feed, and their first message follows in a fresh colour.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-mid-event.webp" alt="An 'Aisha added Lina' event chip in the middle of a group conversation, followed by Lina's first message in her own colour" loading="lazy" width="603" height="300" />
    <figcaption>Someone joining halfway through — one extra System line in the script.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Step 7 — Add events without touching the script</h2>
  <p>
    You don't have to go back to the script to add an event. Hover any message in the preview,
    click the three-dot button, and there's an <strong>Add event below</strong> item — it only
    shows up in group chats. It drops in an event chip reading "Someone joined" below that
    message; click the chip and retype it to whatever you need.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/blog/group-add-event.webp" alt="The message menu in a group chat showing the group-only 'Add event below' option alongside add message, reply, image, date, voice note and call options" loading="lazy" width="324" height="497" />
    <figcaption>"Add event below" appears in the message menu only when the chat is a group.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The rest of that menu works in groups exactly as it does in private chats — replies, images,
    voice notes, call logs and date chips. There's a full walkthrough in
    <a href="/blog/how-to-edit-a-chat-in-the-live-preview">how to edit a chat in the live
    preview</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 8 — Export</h2>
  <p>
    Tick the box confirming the chat is fictional, then <strong>Export PNG</strong> for a
    screenshot or <strong>Export Video</strong> for an animated MP4 where the messages arrive one
    at a time with typing indicators. Both are watermark-free, and the video comes out sized for
    TikTok, Reels and Shorts.
  </p>
