  <h2>Which platforms support groups?</h2>
  <p>
    All six messaging and community platforms do:
    <a href="/fake-whatsapp-chat-generator">WhatsApp</a>,
    <a href="/fake-instagram-dm-generator">Instagram</a>,
    <a href="/fake-messenger-chat-generator">Messenger</a>,
    <a href="/fake-telegram-chat-generator">Telegram</a>,
    <a href="/fake-slack-conversation-generator">Slack</a> and
    <a href="/fake-discord-chat-generator">Discord</a> — each with that platform's own styling
    for member counts and sender names. Switching platform keeps the whole conversation intact.
  </p>
  <p>
    The AI platforms — <a href="/fake-chatgpt-conversation-generator">ChatGPT</a>,
    <a href="/fake-claude-conversation-generator">Claude</a> and
    <a href="/fake-gemini-chat-generator">Gemini</a> — are always one-to-one, since a group
    conversation with an assistant isn't a thing those apps do. Switching to one of them turns
    group mode off.
  </p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <h3>How many people can be in the group?</h3>
  <p>
    As many as you name in the script. Avatar circles draw from seven colours and sender names
    from eight, so a very large group will eventually reuse one. Most good chat stories stay
    under five people anyway — past that, readers lose track of who's who.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I change who "you" are after generating?</h3>
  <p>
    Change the <strong>Your character</strong> dropdown and hit Generate Chat again. That rebuilds
    the conversation from the script, so make script changes before preview edits.
  </p>
  <h3>My chat came out as a private chat instead of a group</h3>
  <p>
    That means only one participant besides you actually speaks. Someone mentioned in a
    <code>System:</code> line but who never sends a message doesn't count — give them at least one
    line.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I use real group photos and names?</h3>
  <p>
    You can upload any image, but don't use a real person's photo or name to make it look like
    they said something they didn't. Fictional characters are safer and usually funnier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ethics">Use it responsibly</h2>
  <p>
    Fake group chats are for <strong>clearly fictional</strong> content — chat stories, memes,
    skits, product demos, presentations and teaching examples. Don't use one to impersonate real
    people, fabricate evidence or mislead anyone. Group chats are the format most likely to be
    screenshotted and passed around out of context, so if there's any chance yours could be taken
    as real, label it as a mockup.
  </p>

